WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:04.390 align:start position:0%
 
welcome back to curious archive  this video is the finale of my two-part  series on the biology of darwin iv an  alien planet explored in the book  expedition by wayne barlow which i have  a link to in the description  on this perilous world deadly predators  strange herbivores and uncategorizable  oddities are still waiting to be  discovered so follow along on this  first-hand account of an expedition  across a spectacular planet a journey  that will end with an encounter with a  mysterious observer  i am now one week into my survey of  darwin iv to the east of the amoebic sea  i encounter the monolithic skull of an  emperor sea strider that appears to have  died long ago i notice a herd of  short-legged herbivores called literal  lopes taking cover at the skull's base  i soon realize what they're hiding from  a shadow swoops overhead  the literal lopes scatter in all  directions in the resulting panic a lone  literal lope is knocked to the ground  with a heavy thud and the shadow swoops  in from above  this aerial predator is called a skewer  and it is the uncontested ruler of the  skies of darwin 4 with a wingspan of  almost 50 feet or 15 meters at a glance  the monstrous skewers almost resemble  biological stealth aircrafts while the  idea of an animal converging on a jet  like shape might seem surprising the  real anatomy of certain birds of prey  and stealth aircrafts are quite similar  due to the universal constraints of  aerodynamics the skewers take this  convergence to a new level however  through their ability to internally  create methane gas which is then  combusted within the four jet pods  located on their wings for propulsion  and when a skewer chooses a target the  hunt is already over i watch as a skewer  rises into the air and pitches downward  with its stinger pointed forwards in an  instant it impales its prey i count  myself lucky i'm too small to be its  target yet as i watch the skewer fly off  i get the strange feeling i'm not alone  even as i continue on i feel like  something is watching me  putting these feelings aside for now  further east i reach a sprawling  flatland where the margins of the  amoebic sea have receded within this  region which was once the sea floor i  spot a sackback a bulky red lifeform  with peculiar anatomy this species gets  their name from a sac used to store  pre-digested food from the amoebic sea  almost like the world's weirdest camel's  hump even stranger however is that this  sackback's stored food isn't for itself  but for its mate under the sand a second  sackback has buried itself up to its  head along with its eggs and relies on  the above ground partner to bring it  food strange as this might sound earth  hornbills exhibit a highly convergent  behavior when a hornbill needs to  incubate its eggs it completely seals  itself within its nest while its mate  dutifully brings it regurgitated food  for a period lasting up to four months  it's a risky endeavor which requires a  tremendous amount of trust between the  two parents so it is strangely moving  that such a social behavior would arise  not just on earth but on a planet as  perilous as darwin 4.  journeying to the south i find myself  face to face with a rather unsightly  creature this is a bladderhorn an animal  that is so bizarre it is at first  difficult to categorize it lives at the  base of an equatorial mountain range and  thankfully seems to feed on plants  rather than other life-forms with their  mid-sized grazing niche and bellowing  calls these creatures are in many ways  the elk of darwin 4.  another bladder horn approaches from  behind the first one isn't happy with  this invader and bellows while it  inflates its glowing horns when this  fails the two butt heads  like birds of paradise these bladder  horns appear to engage in bizarre  displays over territory and mating  rights indeed many birds of paradise  rituals are just as strange as the alien  bladderhorns which is saying something i  decide to leave the bladderhorns to it  and see what other lifeforms dwell in  this mountain range scaling the  foothills of the equatorial mountains i  find myself caught in a heavy rainfall  taking shelter under an overhanging rock  i realized two leathery brown creatures  are huddled nearby also hiding from the  storm these are keeled sliders peaceful  mountainous herbivores which thankfully  aren't aggravated by my presence like  mountain goats keeled sliders can  maneuver down steep slopes with  remarkable ease using their specialized  front limbs yet these keeled sliders are  staying remarkably still a scan detects  20 small life forms in the tiny cave  behind the pair it seems these are young  parents guarding a nest of newborns i  don't want to disrupt them and since the  rain is letting up i'll be on my way  after a grueling climb i reached the  summit of the equatorial mountains  looking to the horizon as i catch my  breath i spot a herd of four-legged  grazers on the peaks beside me these are  spring wings and while they look  ordinary from a distance spring wings  are the champions of the chilly alpine  skies a gust of wind howls overhead i  look up and see an adult spring wing  leaping into the air above me unfurling  gliding appendages that allow it to soar  out into the abyss watching a cow-sized  organism take flight is a majestic and  bizarre sight made possible thanks to  darwin 4's thick atmosphere and low  gravity their fixed wing structure  resembles the body plan of draco lizards  which can glide in a similar manner  although they're much smaller just as  i'm about to depart i notice something  moving on the rock above me but it  quickly recedes from view it seems i am  still being observed heading northwards  i'm drawn to a commotion caused by a  stampeding herd these behemoths are  called uns creatures named for the sound  they make when they exhale through their  dorsal vents heavyset herbivores ants  are the lumbering bison of darwin 4 who  rely on their herd to stay safe while  they browse for plants yet unth herds  aren't without conflict before my eyes  two ants engage in an earth-shaking  battle for dominance throwing their full  weight into each other their posturing  reminds me of muskox clashes on earth  yet despite the occasional clash the  unthird seems to be remarkably  tight-knit as they are united against a  common enemy the weather  this far to the north nothing is more  dangerous than the dropping temperatures  i've now reached a wind-swept region  where the ground is covered in  near-permanent ice and most life forms  on darwin 4 can't survive for long yet  even in this extreme biome some  creatures have found a way to adapt to  the cold  close by i notice an organism which at  first glance appears to be in distress  as it's half buried in the snow at an  awkward angle looking closer however i  realize that this creature called a  tundra plow is actually scouring under  the surface for food plenty of earth  animals forage for food under the ground  and many others tunnel under the snow  for similar reasons but the tundra plow  makes such behaviors look extremely tame  by comparison staying halfway  underground in a manner that looks like  a face plant for long periods it's a  somewhat absurd method of survival but  it appears to be working reasonably well  trekking as far north as i can safely  travel i've reached a glacier cap where  only the most extreme species can live  here i find the shivering ice crawlers  small nondescript organisms that slowly  make their way across the glacier's  surface feeding on microorganisms with a  mouth on the underside of their shell  with their segmented bodies the ice  crawlers have converged on a body plan  similar to giant isopods crustaceans  that can also endure frigid temperatures  i'm about to turn back when perhaps the  strangest life form i've seen dashes  across my field of vision this is a rime  runner a fast-moving creature that hops  over the ice on its single foot while a  single legged animal is highly unusual  it's not altogether unheard of in the  natural world as most bivalve mollusks  like clams also possess only a single  foot which they use as a bizarre form of  motion it's likely that the rime runner  evolved from a similar organism even  stranger than this creature's foot  however is the parachute shaped  structure on the front of its head at  first i thought this orange structure  was a separate life form but it's  actually connected to the rhyme runner  via a thin neural cable looking closely  at the floating organ it seems it's a  sensory packet that helps the rime  runner better navigate through  echolocation almost like an organic  radar dish a unique life form indeed  returning to the warmth of the southern  reaches i worry i won't find creatures  as strange as the rime runner my worries  quickly fade however when i spot two  crescent-shaped lifeforms hovering in  the distance  these wrinkled oddities are rugo's  floaters rather lazy creatures they fly  in wide slow circles a scan tells me  they're able to stay aloft thanks to  internal sacks of buoyant gas  this leads me to wonder if the bulky  rugos floaters can fly using this method  might a true giant be hidden in the  skies  i soon get my answer the largest  airborne creature of darwin 4 reaches  through the clouds this is an ebony  blister wing an animal that is longer  than even the emperor sea striders i  glimpsed earlier in this expedition  although much lighter and more fragile  while i'm only able to glimpse this  creature for a moment i can theorize  that ebony blister wing stays aloft  using the bubble-like bladders on its  wings and body which keep it afloat like  giant balloons and while the diet of  this majestic being is unknown i suspect  it feeds on airborne microscopic  organisms filtering them from the skies  much like a baleen whale filters  zooplankton from the ocean  it is now my final day on darwin 4. just  as i'm returning to my launch site from  the skies descends the most important  life form i've seen  this is an eosapien a highly unusual yet  clearly complex floating organism that  hovers in the air and seems to be  observing me with quiet intelligence i  realize this is my mysterious follower  and it is darwin iv's version of  intelligent life in its hands it holds a  carved defensive weapon not wanting to  alarm the eosapien with any sudden  movements i sit for a while and map out  its anatomy the eu sapiens brain appears  to be housed somewhere in its central  body likely close to its sensory organs  furthermore the eosapien also possesses  flexible digits that enable tool  crafting while right now it seems  eosapiens are only crafting rudimentary  tools perhaps long into the future they  will build a civilization here on darwin  4. for species like the eosapien the  future is full of exciting possibilities  seemingly content with its own  observations the eosapien departs  watching the eosapien leave reminds me  that the time has come for me to also  return home but if you enjoyed this  expedition across darwin 4 as much as i  did i once again encourage you to  purchase the book and check out more of  wayne barlow's incredible work as always  links are in the description also thanks  for watching if you enjoyed this entry  please lend your support to help make  videos like this possible by liking  subscribing and hitting the notification  icon to stay up to date on all things  curious see you in the next video  you

